{"result":[{"biographic_references":[],"contributors":{"authors":[{"aliases":[],"checked":"1","codes":["bender.carl-m"],"name":"Bender, Carl M."},{"aliases":[],"checked":"1","codes":["holm.darryl-d"],"name":"Holm, Darryl D."},{"aliases":[],"checked":"0","codes":["hook.daniel-w"],"name":"Hook, Daniel W."}],"author_references":[],"editors":[]},"database":"Zbl","datestamp":"2007-02-08T10:59:46Z","document_type":{"code":"j","description":"journal article"},"editorial_contributions":[{"language":"English","reviewer":{"author_code":null,"reviewer_id":null,"name":null,"sign":null},"text":"Summary: The motion of a classical pendulum in a gravitational field of strength \\(g\\) is explored. The complex trajectories as well as the real ones are determined. If \\(g\\) is taken to be imaginary, the Hamiltonian that describes the pendulum becomes \\(\\mathcal{PT}\\)-symmetric. The classical motion for this \\(\\mathcal{PT}\\)-symmetric Hamiltonian is examined in detail. We also study the complex motion of this pendulum in the presence of an external periodic forcing term.","contribution_type":"summary"}],"id":5123244,"identifier":"1112.70020","keywords":["complex chaotic motion","periodic forcing term"],"language":{"languages":["English"],"addition":[null]},"license":[],"links":[{"identifier":"10.1088/1751-8113/40/3/F01","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1751-8113/40/3/F01"},{"identifier":"math-ph/0609068","type":"arxiv","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0609068"}],"msc":[{"code":"70K55","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Transition to stochasticity (chaotic behavior) for nonlinear problems in mechanics"},{"code":"70K40","scheme":"msc2020","text":"Forced motions for nonlinear problems in mechanics"}],"references":[],"source":{"book":[],"pages":"F81-F89","series":[{"acronym":null,"issn":[{"number":"1751-8113","type":"print"},{"number":"1751-8121","type":"electronic"}],"issue":"3","issue_id":192427,"parallel_title":null,"part":null,"publisher":"IOP Publishing, Bristol","serie